  "levels": [
    {
      "id": "L1",
      "question": "Do you fully understand how a language model turns text into the next word?",
      "title": "What a language model does",
      "paragraph": "A language model writes by predicting. It splits the text so far into {tokens}, short pieces of words that each map to a number, and passes them through a stack of {transformer layers}. Every layer uses {attention} to weigh each token against every other, so a word is read in the context of the words around it.[1] The last layer turns this into a {probability distribution}: odds for every token in a vocabulary of about 100,000. The model {samples} one of them, and the {temperature} setting decides how adventurous that pick is; a low value almost always takes the likeliest token.[4] The chosen token is appended to the text, and the whole loop runs again on the longer text. Writing a paragraph is this loop repeated, one token at a time.[2]",
      "terms": [
        {
          "term": "tokens",
          "definition": "The pieces a model reads and writes. Common words are one token; rare words are split into several (\"unbelievable\" might be \"un\", \"believ\", \"able\"). Each token is a number in the model's vocabulary.",
          "part": "A1",
          "sources": [
            3
          ]
        },
        {
          "term": "transformer layers",
          "definition": "The repeated blocks that make up the model. Each one takes every token's current meaning and refines it using the others; a large model stacks dozens of them.",
          "part": "A2",
          "sources": [
            1,
            2
          ]
        },
        {
          "term": "attention",
          "definition": "The step inside each layer that decides which other tokens matter for this one. In \"the cat sat on the\", the next word leans on \"sat on\" far more than on \"the\". Attention gives each pair of tokens a weight and mixes their meanings by those weights.",
          "part": "A3",
          "sources": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "term": "probability distribution",
          "definition": "A list of odds, one for every possible next token, that add up to 1. \"mat\" at 0.62 means the model gives it a 62% chance.",
          "part": "A4",
          "sources": [
            2
          ]
        },
        {
          "term": "samples",
          "definition": "Picks one token at random, weighted by the odds, instead of always taking the top one. This is why the same prompt can give different answers.",
          "sources": [
            4
          ]
        },
        {
          "term": "temperature",
          "definition": "A number that sharpens or flattens the odds before the pick. Near 0 the top token nearly always wins; around 1 less likely tokens get real chances.",
          "part": "A5",
          "sources": [
            4
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Vaswani et al., Attention Is All You Need (2017)",
          "kind": "paper",
          "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762"
        },
        {
          "title": "Jay Alammar, The Illustrated Transformer",
          "kind": "page",
